## Proof

Consider $L^{2}(-\pi,\pi)$ with the standard inner product. Recall the classical trigonometric system
$$\{1,\,\cos x,\,\cos 2x,\dots,\,\sin x,\,\sin 2x,\dots\}$$
is complete (total) in $L^{2}(-\pi,\pi)$: every $f\in L^{2}(-\pi,\pi)$ equals its Fourier series in the $L^{2}$-norm.

**Key observation.** Every $\cos nx$ (and the constant $1/2\propto 1$) is an **even** function, and every $\sin nx$ is an **odd** function. Hence:
- the closed linear span of $\{1/2,\cos x,\cos 2x,\dots\}$ is contained in the subspace of even $L^{2}$-functions;
- the closed linear span of $\{\sin x,\sin 2x,\dots\}$ is contained in the subspace of odd $L^{2}$-functions.

We show the reverse inclusions.

---

### Even functions

Let $f\in L^{2}(-\pi,\pi)$ be even: $f(-x)=f(x)$ a.e. Its Fourier sine coefficients are
$$b_n=\frac{1}{\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(x)\sin(nx)\,dx=0\quad(n\ge 1),$$
since $f(x)\sin(nx)$ is odd and integrates to $0$ over the symmetric interval. Thus the Fourier series of $f$ contains only the constant and cosine terms:
$$f=\frac{a_0}{2}+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}a_n\cos(nx)\quad\text{in }L^{2},$$
i.e. $f$ lies in the $L^{2}$-closure of $\operatorname{span}\{1,\cos x,\cos 2x,\dots\}=\operatorname{span}\{1/2,\cos x,\cos 2x,\dots\}$.

Conversely every element of this span is even, and an $L^{2}$-limit of even functions is even. So the closed subspace generated by $\{1/2,\cos x,\cos 2x,\dots\}$ is **exactly the space of even $L^{2}$-functions**.

---

### Odd functions

Let $g\in L^{2}(-\pi,\pi)$ be odd: $g(-x)=-g(x)$ a.e. Its constant and cosine coefficients are
$$a_0=\frac{1}{\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}g(x)\,dx=0,\qquad a_n=\frac{1}{\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}g(x)\cos(nx)\,dx=0\quad(n\ge 1),$$
since $g(x)\cos(nx)$ (and $g$ itself) are odd. Hence
$$g=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}b_n\sin(nx)\quad\text{in }L^{2},$$
so $g$ lies in the $L^{2}$-closure of $\operatorname{span}\{\sin x,\sin 2x,\dots\}$.

Conversely every element of this span is odd, and an $L^{2}$-limit of odd functions is odd. So the closed subspace generated by $\{\sin x,\sin 2x,\dots\}$ is **exactly the space of odd $L^{2}$-functions**.

---

### Orthogonal decomposition

Every $f\in L^{2}(-\pi,\pi)$ decomposes uniquely as
$$f=f_e+f_o,\qquad f_e(x)=\frac{f(x)+f(-x)}{2}\ \text{(even)},\quad f_o(x)=\frac{f(x)-f(-x)}{2}\ \text{(odd)},$$
and $f_e\perp f_o$ since the product of an even and an odd function is odd. Therefore
$$L^{2}(-\pi,\pi)=\overline{\operatorname{span}}\{1/2,\cos x,\cos 2x,\dots\}\;\oplus\;\overline{\operatorname{span}}\{\sin x,\sin 2x,\dots\},$$
an orthogonal direct sum of the even and odd subspaces.

---

### Conclusion

- The closed subspace generated by $\left\{\dfrac{1}{2},\cos x,\cos 2x,\dots\right\}$ consists of **all even functions** in $L^{2}(-\pi,\pi)$, i.e. those $f$ with $f(-x)=f(x)$ a.e.
- The closed subspace generated by $\{\sin x,\sin 2x,\dots\}$ consists of **all odd functions** in $L^{2}(-\pi,\pi)$, i.e. those $f$ with $f(-x)=-f(x)$ a.e.

These two closed subspaces are orthogonal complements of each other, and their direct sum is all of $L^{2}(-\pi,\pi)$.

